~ Groups — many things of one declared shape.
~
~ A container names one value. A group names a shape and holds many of them, so a
~ program can finally say "every body" instead of naming Body One, Body Two and
~ repeating itself. Fields carry the same types and bounds a container does: a
~ guard that holds for one element holds for all of them, because the bound is a
~ property of the shape rather than of any instance.
create group "Bodies" with
  "X" of type int,
  "Y" of type int,
  "Velocity Y" of type int

~ Values are positional, in the order the fields are declared just above.
insert element 10, 0, 0 to Bodies
insert element 20, 5, 3 to Bodies

print(count(Bodies))          ~ 2

~ A cursor walks the group. It is the loop's own state — like a loop-local
~ container, it is never named in a footprint; the GROUP is what gets declared.
~ Reads use `from` and writes name the target with `of`, the same asymmetry a
~ class already has.
create action "ApplyGravity" reads Bodies writes Bodies as
  loop over Bodies as Body
    update Velocity Y of Body to [Velocity Y from Body + 10]
    update Y of Body to [Y from Body + Velocity Y from Body]
  end
end

ApplyGravity()
ApplyGravity()
ApplyGravity()

loop over Bodies as Body
  print(Y from Body)          ~ 60, 74
end

~ Two cursors over the same group is how a pair test is written — every body
~ against every other, which is what collision detection needs. The inner cursor
~ cannot clobber the outer one.
loop over Bodies as First
  loop over Bodies as Second
    print([[X from First * 100] + X from Second])
  end
end
